Use g_memdup2 in pa_proplist_set

The build emitted a deprecation warning on every compile:

    apulse-proplist.c:82:5: warning: 'g_memdup' is deprecated:
        Use 'g_memdup2' instead [-Wdeprecated-declarations]

Not just noise. pa_proplist_set takes nbytes as a size_t:

    pa_proplist_set(pa_proplist *p, const char *key, const void *data,
                    size_t nbytes)
    ...
        v->data = g_memdup(data, nbytes);

g_memdup's size parameter is a guint, so on 64-bit the call narrows. That is why
glib deprecated it: a caller passing more than 4 GB gets a short copy and a heap
overflow. g_memdup2 takes a gsize and is the documented replacement.

Not reachable here, since the only proplists apulse handles are small key/value
sets, but it is a one-line correction and a warning nobody ever fixes is a
warning everybody learns to ignore.

Guarded on GLIB_CHECK_VERSION because g_memdup2 appeared in glib 2.68 and apulse
declares no minimum glib version of its own:

    pkg_check_modules(REQ glib-2.0 alsa REQUIRED)

Debian Bookworm, the pinned build base, ships 2.74, so the guard is belt and
braces rather than a live path here. It keeps the patch applicable to an older
glib rather than trading one broken build for another.

The only call site in the tree; verified with grep across src/.

Independent of 0001 and 0002. Applies on top of either or neither.

--- a/src/apulse-proplist.c
+++ b/src/apulse-proplist.c
@@ -79,7 +79,15 @@
     if (!v)
         return -1;
 
+    // g_memdup takes a guint, but nbytes here is a size_t, so the call also
+    // narrows on 64-bit. g_memdup2 takes a gsize and is the documented
+    // replacement. Guarded because it appeared in glib 2.68 and apulse itself
+    // declares no minimum version; Debian Bookworm ships 2.74.
+#if GLIB_CHECK_VERSION(2, 68, 0)
+    v->data = g_memdup2(data, nbytes);
+#else
     v->data = g_memdup(data, nbytes);
+#endif
     v->len = nbytes;
 
     g_hash_table_insert(p->ht, strdup(key), v);
